--- abstract

DomainKeys Identified Mail v2 (DKIM2) permits a person, role, or
organization that owns a signing domain to document that it has
handled an email message by associating their domain with the
message.  This is achieved by providing a hash value that has
been calculated on the current contents of the message and then applying a cryptographic
signature that covers the hash values and other details about the
transmission of the message. Verification is performed by querying an entry
within the signing domain's DNS space to retrieve an appropriate public
key. As a message is transferred from author to recipient, systems that
alter the body or header fields will provide details of their changes
and calculate new hash values. Further signatures will be added to
provide a "chain" that receivers can validate. This permits verifiers
to identify the nature of changes made by intermediaries and apply a
reputation to the systems that made changes. DKIM2 also allows
recipients to detect when messages have been unexpectedly "replayed"
and will ensure that Delivery Status Notifications are only sent
to entities that were involved in the transmission of a message.

--- middle

# Introduction

DomainKeys Identified Mail v2 (DKIM2) permits a person, role, or
organization to document that they have handled an email message by
associating a domain name [RFC1034] with the message [RFC5322]. A
public key signature is used to record that they have been able
to read the contents of the message and write to it.

Verification of claims is achieved by fetching a public key stored
in the DNS under the relevant domain and then checking the signature.

Message transit from author to recipient is through
Forwarders that typically make no substantive change to the message
content and thus preserve the DKIM2 signature. Where they do make
a change the changes they have made are documented so that
these can be "undone" and the original signature validated.

When a message is forwarded from one system to another an
additional DKIM2 signature is added on each occasion. This Chain
of Custody assists Verifiers in distinguishing between messages that
were intended to be sent to a particular email address and those
that are being "replayed" to that address.

The Chain of Custody can also be used to ensure that delivery status
notifications are only sent to entities that were involved in the
transmission of a message.

Organizations that process a message can add to their signature
a request for feedback as to any opinion (for example, that the
email was considered to be spam) that the eventual recipient of
the message wishes to share.

## DKIM2 architecture documents

Readers are advised to be familiar with the material in TBA, TBA and TBA
which provide the background for the development of DKIM2, an overview
of the service, and deployment and operations guidance and advice.

# Terminology and definitions

This section defines terms used in the rest of the document.

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL
NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED",
"MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as
described in BCP 14 {{RFC2119}} {{RFC8174}} when, and only when, they
appear in all capitals, as shown here.

DKIM2 is designed to operate within the Internet Mail service, as
defined in [RFC5598].  Basic email terminology is taken from that
specification.

DKIM2 inherits many ideas from DKIM ([RFC6376]) which, for clarity
we refer to in this specification as DKIM1. In addition, some features
were influenced by the experimental ARC protocol ([RFC8617]) and
the lessons learned from that experiment ([CONCLUDEARC]).

Syntax descriptions use Augmented BNF (ABNF) [RFC5234], together with
the %s"..." notation for case-sensitive strings defined in [RFC7405].

This document uses JSON [RFC8259] to encode the Recipes which
record changes made to the message header fields or the message body.
The JSON objects are then base64 encoded. This means that a
standard JSON parser can be used to create what may be quite
complex data structures. Keys in the top-level JSON object other
than "h" and "b" are reserved for future specifications, which may
define new kinds of Recipe with their own syntax. If present,
unrecognized top-level keys will be ignored. Within "h" and "b" only
the structures defined in this document are permitted; in particular
each Recipe step MUST contain exactly one of the keys "c", "d" or "u".

## Signer

Elements in the mail system that sign messages on behalf of a domain
are referred to as Signers.  These may be MUAs (Mail User Agents),
MSAs (Mail Submission Agents), MTAs (Mail Transfer Agents), or other
agents such as mailing list "exploders".  In general, any Signer will
be involved in the injection of a message into the message system in
some way.  The key point is that a message is signed before it
leaves the administrative domain of the Signer.

## Forwarder

[RFC5598] defines a Relay as transmitting or retransmitting a message
but states that it will not modify the envelope information or the
message content semantics. It also defines a Gateway as a hybrid of
User and Relay that connects heterogeneous mail services. In this
document we use the concept of a Forwarder which is an MTA that receives
a message and then, as an alternative to delivering it into a
destination mailbox, can forward it on to another system in an
automated, pre-determined, manner.

## Reviser

As will be seen, a Forwarder may alter the message content or header
fields, in such a way that existing signatures on the message will
no longer validate. If so, then a record will be made of these
changes. We call a Forwarder that makes such changes a Reviser.

## Verifier

Elements in the mail system that verify signatures are referred to as
Verifiers.  These may be Forwarders, Revisers, MTAs, Mail Delivery
Agents (MDAs), or MUAs.
It is an expectation of DKIM2 that a recipient of a message will
wish to verify some or all signatures before determining whether or
not to accept the message or pass it on to another entity.

## Signing Domain

A domain name associated with a signature. This domain may be
associated with the author of an email, their organization, a
company hired to deliver the email, a mailing list operator, or
some other entity that handles email. What they have in common is
that at some point they had access to the entire contents of the
email and were in a position to add their signature to the email.

## Originator

The entity that creates and sends the initial form of a message.
The Originator adds the first Message-Instance header field (m=1) and
the first DKIM2-Signature header field (i=1) to the message.

## Header field

As defined in [RFC5322], a header field is a single logical line in
the message header consisting of a field name, a colon, and a field
body (value).  In this document "header field" always refers to a
single field; "header fields" (plural) refers to multiple fields.
The unqualified term "header" is avoided to prevent ambiguity.

## Tag

A named element within a header field (see {{hfMessageInstance}}
and {{hfDKIM2signature}}).  A tag
consists of a tag-name and a tag-value separated by an equals sign.
Tags are separated by semicolons within the header field.

## Message body

The content of an email message that follows the blank line after
the header fields, treated as a sequence of octets.  In this document,
the terms "body" and "message body" are used interchangeably.

## Hash

A fixed-length value produced by applying a cryptographic hash
function (such as SHA-256) to an input.  DKIM2 uses hashes to
create a compact, verifiable representation of message header fields
and the message body.

## Glossary

The following terms are used throughout this document:

DKIM1
: The original DomainKeys Identified Mail protocol as specified in
[RFC6376].

DKIM Replay
: An attack in which messages with valid signatures but with malicious
content are sent from third party systems at scale, with the intent
that the validity of the signature will ensure they are delivered.

DKIM2-Signature
: A header field containing a cryptographic signature over the
Message-Instance and DKIM2-Signature header fields of a message,
along with metadata about the Signing Domain, SMTP envelope, and
timestamp.

Message-Instance
: A header field containing cryptographic hashes of the message
header fields and body, along with optional Recipes that permit
the reversal of changes made at that hop.

Recipe
: A set of instructions encoded as a JSON object within the r= tag
of a Message-Instance header field.  Recipes allow a Verifier to
reconstruct the previous state of a message from its current state,
by specifying which parts of the header fields or body to copy and
which literal values to substitute.

Chain of Custody
: The sequence of DKIM2-Signature header fields on a message, each
recording the SMTP envelope addresses (MAIL FROM and RCPT TO) used
at each hop.  A valid Chain of Custody demonstrates that the message
followed a plausible path from Originator to the current recipient.

Selector
: A subdivision of the key namespace for a Signing Domain, used
to look up the public key in DNS.  The Selector value is combined
with the Signing Domain to form the DNS query name; for example,
Selector "foo.bar" and Signing Domain "example.com" give
"foo.bar._domainkey.example.com".

Authentic
: A From header field is Authentic if, when it is placed into a message
(either at message creation or later modification), the next
DKIM2-Signature header field created for the message uses a Signing
Domain that can be matched to the domain within the email
address in the From.

Validly Signed DKIM2 Message
: A Validly Signed DKIM2 Message is one which meets all the
requirements set out in this specification on Signers, particularly
those for generating DKIM2-Signature and Message-Instance header
fields. Further, when transmitted, the SMTP MAIL FROM and RCPT TO
will match the values recorded in the most recent DKIM2-Signature
header field.

Provisionally Verified
: A message is said to be Provisionally Verified if a cryptographic
check shows that the hash and signature values in the highest numbered
Message-Instance and DKIM2-Signature header fields correspond
with the message to hand. This is a precondition for the message
being a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message but not a guarantee that it is.

## Whitespace

There are two forms of whitespace used in this specification:

*  WSP represents simple whitespace, i.e., a space or a tab character
   (formal definition in [RFC5234]).

*  FWS is folding whitespace.  It allows multiple lines separated by
   CRLF followed by at least one whitespace, to be joined.

The formal ABNF for these are (WSP given for information only):

    WSP =   SP / HTAB
    FWS =   [*WSP CRLF] 1*WSP

The definition of FWS is identical to that in [RFC5322] except for
the exclusion of obs-FWS.

## Imported ABNF tokens

The following tokens are imported from other RFCs as noted.  Those
RFCs are definitive.

The following tokens are imported from [RFC5321]:

*  "Domain"

*  "Forward-path"

*  "reverse-path"

The following tokens are imported from [RFC5322]:

*  "field-name" (name of a header field)

Other tokens not defined herein are imported from [RFC5234].  These
are intuitive primitives such as SP, HTAB, WSP, ALPHA, DIGIT, CRLF,
etc.

## Common ABNF tokens {#common-ABNF}

The following ABNF tokens are used elsewhere in this document:

    ALPHADIGITD  = (ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" / "_")

    textstring   =  [FWS] ALPHADIGITD *(ALPHADIGITD) [FWS]

    ALPHADIGITPS =  (FWS / ALPHA / DIGIT / "+" / "/")

    base64string =  ALPHADIGITPS *(ALPHADIGITPS) [[FWS] "=" [[FWS] "="]]

Note that base64strings are defined in [RFC4648], but that document
does not contain any ABNF. A base64string MUST be padded with zero
bits and trailing = characters provided if needed. This allows
implementations to compare base64string values without decoding
them.

Note that the definition of base64string allows
for the presence of FWS, which simplifies folding header fields
to an allowable line length. FWS within base64strings will be
ignored when their value is being used.

Values that match textstring, such as the names of hash algorithms,
signature algorithms and flags that may be defined in the future, are
case-sensitive.

# Signing and verification cryptographic algorithms {#algorithms}

DKIM2 supports multiple hashing and digital signature algorithms.
Two hashing algorithms (SHA256 and SHA512) and three signing algorithms
(RSA-SHA256, Ed25519-SHA256 and MLDSA44-Ed25519-SHA512) are defined in
this specification. Signers MAY implement either or both of the two
hashing algorithms and any or all of the signing algorithms. Verifiers
MUST implement all five algorithms.

## The SHA256 and SHA512 hashing algorithms

The SHA256 hashing algorithm may be used to compute body and header
hashes as defined in {{computing-body-hash}} and
{{computing-header-hash}}. As an alternative SHA512 may be used.
Both hash functions are specified in {{FIPS-180-4}}.

The resultant values are identified by the text string "sha256" or
"sha512" as appropriate and are placed into Message-Instance header
fields.

## The RSA-SHA256 signing algorithm

The RSA-SHA256 signing algorithm computes a hash over all the Message-Instance
and DKIM2-Signature header fields as described in {{calculate-signature}} using
SHA-256 {{FIPS-180-4}}. That hash is then signed by the Signer using the RSA
algorithm (defined in PKCS#1 version 1.5 [RFC8017]) supplied with the Signer's
private key. The hash MUST NOT be truncated or converted into any
form other than the native binary form before being signed.  The
signing algorithm MUST use a public exponent of 65537.

Signers MUST use RSA keys of at least 1024 bits.  Verifiers MUST be able
to validate signatures with keys ranging from 1024 bits to 2048 bits, and
they MAY be able to validate signatures with larger keys.

The signature value (expressed in base64) is placed (with the identifying
text string "rsa-sha256") into DKIM2-Signature header fields.

## The Ed25519-SHA256 signing algorithm

The Ed25519-SHA256 signing algorithm computes a hash over all the Message-Instance
and DKIM2-Signature fields as described in {{calculate-signature}} using
SHA-256 {{FIPS-180-4}} as the hash-alg. It signs the hash with the PureEdDSA
variant Ed25519, as defined in Section 5.1 of [RFC8032].

The signature value (expressed in base64) is placed (with the identifying
text string "ed25519-sha256") into DKIM2-Signature header fields.

## The MLDSA44-Ed25519-SHA512 signing algorithm

The MLDSA44-Ed25519-SHA512 signing algorithm computes a hash over all the
Message-Instance and DKIM2-Signature fields as described in
{{calculate-signature}} using SHA-512 {{FIPS-180-4}} as the hash-alg.
It signs the hash with both the "post-quantum" MLDSA-44 algorithm
{{FIPS-204}} and the PureEdDSA variant Ed25519 (see [RFC8032]). The signature
value is the MLDSA-44 value immediately followed by the Ed25519 value.

The signature value (expressed in base64) is placed (with the identifying
text string "mldsa44-ed25519-sha512") into DKIM2-Signature header fields.

## Other algorithms

Other algorithms MAY be defined in the future. A hash or signature
that a Verifier does not implement will of necessity be disregarded.
Signers will need to keep this in mind when choosing the algorithms
that they will use.

## Selectors

To support multiple concurrent public keys per Signing Domain, the
key namespace is subdivided using Selectors.

The number of public keys and corresponding Selectors for each domain
is determined by the domain owner. Many domain owners will use just one
Selector, whereas administratively distributed organizations can choose
to manage disparate Selectors
and key pairs in different regions or on different email servers.
Selectors can also be used to delegate a signing authority, which
can be withdrawn at any time. Selectors also make it possible to
seamlessly replace keys on a routine basis by signing with a new
Selector, while keeping the key associated with the old Selector
available.

Periods are allowed in Selectors and are component separators. Periods in
Selectors define DNS label boundaries in a manner similar to the
conventional use in domain names.  This will allow portions of
the Selector namespace to be delegated.

ABNF:

    selector = Domain

## Key management {#key_management}

Some level of assurance is required that
a public key is associated with the claimed Signer. DKIM2
does this by fetching the key from the DNS for the Signing Domain
specified in the d= field of the DKIM2-Signature header field.

DKIM2 keys are stored in a subdomain named "_domainkey".  Given a
DKIM2-Signature field with a "d=" tag of "example.com" and a Selector
of "foo.bar", the DNS query will be for "foo.bar._domainkey.example.com".

NOTE: these keys are no different, and are stored in the same locations
as those for DKIM1 ([RFC6376]).

Further details can be found in [DKIMKEYS].

# Unsigned header fields {#ignoreheaders}

As explained in detail below, DKIM2 will provide a cryptographic
signature for all header fields that are present within a message,
whether they have a standardized meaning or are just placed there
at the whim of a particular sender.

In order to simplify message handling some header fields are not signed
and processing of the message by Signers and Verifiers will be performed
as if they were not present.

The exceptions fall into three classes:

 1. Trace header fields

    "Received" or "Return-Path" header fields are ignored. These are
    trace header fields as described in [RFC5321] and serve only to document
    details of the SMTP transmission process.

    The experimental "Delivered-To:" header field ([RFC9228]) is also
    ignored.

    Other specifications designate further header fields as trace
    fields. A survey of such specifications, using the methodology of
    [HDRMAINT], identifies "Auto-Submitted", "DL-Expansion-History",
    "Original-Recipient", "SIO-Label-History", "VBR-Info" and
    "X400-Trace", which are also ignored.

    The same survey records header fields which are added by handling
    agents in transit even though no specification designates them as
    trace fields: "Apparently-To" and "X400-Received". For DKIM2 it is
    that behavior, rather than any formal designation, which matters,
    so these are also ignored.

    Any header field whose field name starts with "Received-", such as
    "Received-SPF" ([RFC7208]), is ignored. This allows future
    specifications to create new trace header fields of this form
    without affecting DKIM2 processing.

 1. Other email authentication mechanisms

    "DKIM-Signature" header fields and the "ARC-Authentication-Results",
    "ARC-Message-Signature" and "ARC-Seal" header fields ([RFC8617])
    are ignored.

    Not including DKIM1 and ARC header fields means that systems that
    wish to add other types of signature as well as a DKIM2 signature
    are free to do this in any convenient order.

 1. Header fields with purely local meaning

    "Authentication-Results" header fields are ignored. These header
    fields are defined in [RFC8601] where it is made clear that their
    contents are only intended for use within the "trust boundary"
    of an "Administrative Management Domain". As they can only be used
    reliably within that trust boundary there has been no need to
    secure them cryptographically and given how they may be added or
    removed as messages cross trust boundaries it is inconvenient and
    unnecessary for DKIM2 to sign them.

    Header fields with a header field name starting with "X-" are
    ignored. Currently deployed email systems use these fields as
    proprietary trace header fields or for other proprietary reasons. They
    have no defined meaning for other systems and it considerably
    simplifies reporting on changes to header fields to ignore them. Note
    that the recommendations in [RFC6648] mean that it is most
    unlikely that any header field name starting "X-" will be developed
    within the IETF.

## Summary

These header fields are not signed:

    Apparently-To:
    ARC-Authentication-Results:
    ARC-Message-Signature:
    ARC-Seal:
    Authentication-Results:
    Auto-Submitted:
    Delivered-To:
    DKIM-Signature:
    DL-Expansion-History:
    Original-Recipient:
    Received:
    Received-*:
    Return-Path:
    SIO-Label-History:
    VBR-Info:
    X-*:
    X400-Received:
    X400-Trace:

# Recipes {#JSONrecipe}

A set of Recipes is used to recreate the previous version of the body
and/or header fields of a message. The Recipes are provided
within a JSON object with the schema:

    {
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "$id": "https://dkim2.org/schemas/recipe-v1",
      "title": "DKIM2 Recipes",
      "description": "See draft-ietf-dkim-dkim2-spec",
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "h": {
          "description": "Recipes to recreate specific header fields",
          "type": "object",
          "minProperties": 1,
          "additionalProperties": { "$ref": "#/$defs/recipe-steps" }
        },
        "b": {
          "description": "Recipes to recreate the body",
          "oneOf": [
            {
              "description": "Body Recipes",
              "$ref": "#/$defs/recipe-steps"
            },
            {
              "description": "Previous body state cannot be recreated",
              "type": "null"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "anyOf": [
        { "required": ["h"] },
        { "required": ["b"] }
      ],
      "$defs": {
        "recipe-steps": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "oneOf": [
              {
                "description": "Copy lines/fields, start to end",
                "type": "object",
                "properties": {
                  "c": { "type": "array",
                    "items": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1 },
                    "minItems": 2, "maxItems": 2
                  }
                },
                "required": ["c"], "additionalProperties": false
              },
              {
                "description": "Data lines/values to emit",
                "type": "object",
                "properties": {
                  "d": { "type": "array",
                    "items": { "$ref": "#/$defs/utf8-line" },
                    "minItems": 1
                  }
                },
                "required": ["d"], "additionalProperties": false
              },
              {
                "description": "Non UTF8 lines/values to emit",
                "type": "object",
                "properties": {
                  "u": { "type": "array",
                    "items": { "$ref": "#/$defs/base64-line" },
                    "minItems": 1
                  }
                },
                "required": ["u"], "additionalProperties": false
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "utf8-line": {
          "description": "One line of text, without CR or LF",
          "type": "string",
          "allOf": [
            { "pattern": "^[^\r\n]*$" }
          ]
        },
        "base64-line": {
          "description": "Canonical base64 of one line",
          "type": "string", "contentEncoding": "base64",
          "allOf": [
            { "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9+/]*={0,2}$" },
            { "pattern": "^(.{4})*$" },
            { "pattern": "^([^=]*|.*[AQgw]==|.*[AEIMQUYcgkosw048]=)$" }
          ]
        }
      }
    }

Note that the specification of JSON schemas is maintained by the JSON Schema
organization, and the relevant specification document is linked to by the
$schema field in each JSON schema.

The JSON keys (property names) MUST be in lower case and they MUST
be unique within any JSON object.

Strings in JSON are always composed of UTF-8 characters. Breaking out
the "utf8-line" into a definition allows the schema to apply a pattern
to it. This pattern ensures that the strings do not contain CR or LF characters.

Since it may be necessary for a Recipe to deal with text that is not valid
UTF-8, the "u" step is provided as an alternative to a "d" step. The pattern
provided in the schema ensures that each string is a valid
"base64string" as set out in {{common-ABNF}} including the restrictions
on padding.

However, there is one difference from "base64string" in that the schema does
not allow FWS to appear within the string. This does not create any practical
issues because the JSON object will be base64 encoded before being
placed into a header field, and that encoded value can have FWS and
hence can be folded in order to conform to header field length limits.

Note that the restrictions on CR and LF characters apply to "u" step
strings (once they are decoded) just as for "d", but this restriction
cannot be expressed within the schema.

It will be noted that the strings in a "u" step will be encoded twice.
To avoid unnecessary extra processing a "u" step SHOULD NOT be used
when a "d" step would suffice.

## Header Recipes {#header-recipes}

A Header Recipe is an array of instructions applied to the specified
header fields with the given header field name. These Recipes
are applied in order to the message which has been received
so as to recreate the message as it was before modifications were made.
Recipes MUST be provided for any relevant header field (see {{ignoreheaders}}
for header fields that are not relevant) that is changed; i.e. it is
not possible to indicate that the previous state of the header fields
cannot be recreated.

If there is no "h" field in the JSON object then there was no
modification to the header fields.

Header field names in the JSON keys are matched with the message
without regard to case. Recipes MUST NOT be specified for header
fields that will not be signed (see {{ignoreheaders}}). A header
field name MUST NOT be present more than once.

If a header field name is not present in the JSON object then all
header fields with that header field name are to be retained.

If the Recipe array for a header field name that is present in the
JSON object is empty then all instances of that header field are to
be removed to reinstate the previous state of the message.

Header fields are numbered "bottom up" (the opposite direction to
the body lines). That is to say, when walking the header fields
from the top of the message to the end of the header fields then
the last header field instance
encountered with any particular header field name is numbered 1,
the header field (with the same header field name) above that is
numbered 2, and so on.

The header fields MUST be counted as if they were unfolded
(in the normal [RFC5322] manner). That is, all
of the physical lines that form a single header field are
processed under the same logical number.

The Recipes are processed in order and the resulting header
fields are emitted so that later header fields will appear above
earlier header fields in the recreated message.

Each Recipe step is a JSON object with exactly one key:

A "c" step has the form {"c": \[start, end\]}. The relevant header field
instances numbered from start to end inclusive, are to be emitted.
The start value of each "c" step MUST be in ascending order and
MUST be greater than the end value of all preceding "c" steps
for this header field name. The end value MUST NOT be less than
the start value. If they are equal, just one header field instance
is to be emitted.

A "d" step has the form {"d": \["value1", "value2", ...\]}. Each
string in the array is treated as a value to which the
relevant header field name and a colon is prepended and a CRLF
is appended and the resultant string is then emitted. Note that
the way in which hashes are calculated (see {{computing-header-hash}})
means that no heed needs to be taken of wrapping
or the case of the header field name. The text strings MUST NOT
contain CR or LF characters. If a string is empty then the
CRLF will immediately follow the header field name and colon.

A "u" step is semantically identical to a "d" step except that
each string value is in base64 and will be decoded before use.
Since the values in a "d" step MUST be valid UTF-8, a "u" step
MUST be used if that is not the case.

## Body Recipes {#body-recipes}

A Body Recipe is an array of instructions applied to the message
body which can recreate the message as it was before modifications
were made.

If there is no "b" field in the JSON object then there was no
modification to the message body. Note that the JSON schema
requires either "h" or "b" to be present.

If the "b" field is null (there are no Recipes) then the previous
state of the message body
cannot be recreated. Verifiers of the message may be able to
determine, by seeing which entity makes this declaration, that
this is acceptable to them because, for example, that entity
is providing a contractually arranged service.

Body lines are numbered "top down" (the opposite direction to
the header fields). The first line of the body (immediately after
the blank line that indicates that there are no more header fields)
is numbered 1.

The Recipes are processed in order and the resulting body lines
are emitted so that later lines will appear below earlier lines
in the recreated message.

Each Recipe step is a JSON object with exactly one key:

A "c" step has the form {"c": \[start, end\]}. The message body lines
from start to end, inclusive, are to be emitted. The start value of
each "c" step MUST be in ascending order and MUST be greater than the
end value of all preceding "c" steps. The end value MUST NOT be less than
the start value. If they are equal, just one message body line
is to be emitted.

A "d" step has the form {"d": \["line1", "line2", ...\]}. Each
string in the array has a CRLF
appended and the resultant string is emitted. The text strings MUST NOT
contain CR or LF characters. If a string is empty then just
a CRLF is emitted.

A "u" step is semantically identical to a "d" step except that
each string value is in base64 and will be decoded before use.
Since the values in a "d" step MUST be valid UTF-8, a "u" step
MUST be used if that is not the case.

# Message hash values {#messagehashes}

A set of cryptographic "hashes" is used to record the current
message body and header fields. The hashes are placed into the
h= tag of a Message-Instance header field.

Since Message-Instance header fields are ignored when calculating the
header hash value, the body hash and header hash may be calculated in
any convenient order.

## Computing the body hash {#computing-body-hash}

The body of messages is treated as merely a string of octets. DKIM2
messages can be either in plain-text or in MIME format; no special
treatment is afforded to MIME content. Message attachments in MIME
format MUST be included in the content that is signed.

The DKIM2 body hash is calculated in the same manner as DKIM1's "simple"
scheme:

All empty lines at the end of the message body are ignored. An empty line
is a line of zero length after removal of the line terminator.  If there
is no body or no trailing CRLF on the message body, a CRLF is added. That
is "*CRLF" at the end of the body is converted to "CRLF".

No other changes are made to the body, which is then processed by the
relevant hash algorithm(s). The name of the hash and the hash value
(converted to base64 form) is then inserted into (Signers) or compared
to (Verifiers) the value of the "h=" tag of the Message-Instance header
field that is being created/verified. If multiple hashes are calculated
then multiple entries within the "h=" value will be inserted/compared.

## Computing the header fields hash {#computing-header-hash}

The header fields hash calculation done by a Signer MUST apply the
following steps in the order given. A Verifier will need to do the
equivalent steps in order to check that the hash they have received
is correct.

*  Ignore some header fields

   When calculating the header field hash some header fields
   are entirely ignored. See {{ignoreheaders}} for the list of header
   field names that MUST be ignored.

   When calculating the header field hash any "Message-Instance" or
   "DKIM2-Signature" header fields MUST be ignored. These header
   fields will be included in the hash value that will be signed
   by a DKIM2-Signature header field and it simplifies implementations
   if they are not included twice, especially when determining
   whether all modifications to a message have been correctly declared.

*  Convert all header field names (not the header field values) to
   lowercase.  For example, convert "SUBJect: AbC" to "subject: AbC".

*  Unfold all header field continuation lines as described in
   [RFC5322]; in particular, lines with terminators embedded in
   continued header field values (that is, CRLF sequences followed by
   WSP) MUST be interpreted without the CRLF.  Implementations MUST
   NOT remove the CRLF at the end of the header field value.

*  Convert all sequences of one or more WSP characters to a single SP
   character.  WSP characters here include those before and after a
   line folding boundary.

*  Delete all WSP characters at the end of each unfolded header field
   value.

*  Delete any WSP characters remaining before and after the colon
   separating the header field name from the header field value.  The
   colon separator MUST be retained.

*  Place the header fields in alphabetical order by the header field
   name.

*  If there is more than one header field with the same header field name
   then the header fields are placed in the order in which they were
   would have been placed into the message header, that is from
   the last within the header upward (the same ordering as is used
   in the header Recipes (see {{header-recipes}})).

   It is sometimes suggested that some MTAs re-order
   header fields after they receive an email. If an MTA does change the
   order of header fields with the same header field name (and those
   header fields will be included in the hash calculation) then it is their
   responsibility to recover the original order
   before verifying an existing signature or passing a previously signed
   message to another MTA that may wish to do such verification.

*  The hash(es) of the concatenated header fields are calculated.

The name of the hash and the hash value
(converted to base64 form) is then inserted into (Signers) or compared
to (Verifiers) the value of the "h=" tag of the Message-Instance header
field that is being created/verified. If multiple hashes are calculated
then multiple entries within the "h=" value will be inserted/compared.

# The relaxed domain match algorithm {#relaxed-domain-match}

The relaxed domain match algorithm is used for several purposes
in the DKIM2 protocol. The match algorithm is asymmetric in that
attention needs to be paid to which value is "first" (Parameter1)
and which is "second" (Parameter2).

The aim is to allow a Signing Domain to be able to sign not only for
itself but also for sub-domains. As well as simplifying key handling
for organizations that use sub-domains, this also allows existing
"bounce-handling" mechanisms that utilize sub-domains to continue
to work alongside DKIM2.

The algorithm operates in such a way that there is no need to consider
issues such as the different numbers of labels in so-called
"public suffixes". The organization operating the "suffix" can determine
whether they are part of the DKIM2 ecosystem or not. In practice, few if
any "Top Level Domains" will act as a Signing Domain.

Where the algorithm has to match rt= or mf= values from a DKIM2-Signature
header field the values are base64 encoded. The description of the
algorithm assumes that they have been decoded.

## Uses of the relaxed domain match algorithm {#rdm-uses}

The relaxed domain match algorithm is used for four purposes:

1. {:#cc-rdm} To assist in addressing the DKIM Replay problem DKIM2 provides a
   Chain of Custody for every message. There are several different cases
   in checking the Chain of Custody where a relaxed domain match
   is required.

   The i=1 DKIM2-Signature header field is not checked, nor is the
   highest numbered one with the "break" flag (see {{verification}}) or
   any below it, nor is any DKIM2-Signature header field that follows
   one with an nd= tag. The
   remaining DKIM2-Signature header fields can be considered in ascending
   numeric order.

   *  If the mf= value recorded in a DKIM2-Signature header field is not
      null then a match is sought between the mf= value and a recipient
      address in the next lower numbered DKIM2-Signature header field.

      The mf= value is Parameter1 for the algorithm,
      the rt= value is Parameter2.

   *  If the mf= value is null (<>) then a match is sought between the
      Signing Domain (d=) and a recipient address in the next lower
      numbered DKIM2-Signature header field.

      The rt= value is Parameter1 for the algorithm,
      the d= value is Parameter2.

   *  A DKIM2-Signature header field with an nd= tag has no mf= or
      rt= tag. A match is sought between the Signing Domain (d=) and
      a recipient address in the next lower numbered DKIM2-Signature
      header field.

      The rt= value is Parameter1 for the algorithm,
      the d= value is Parameter2.

      The next numbered DKIM2-Signature header field will not be checked
      with this algorithm. Instead its d= value is required to be exactly
      the same as the nd= value (see {{imaginary-hops}}).

1. It is necessary to check all the DKIM2-Signature header fields for a match
   between the Signing Domain (specified in the d= tag) and the MAIL FROM
   domain (mf=).

   The (decoded) mf= value is Parameter1 for the algorithm, the d= value
   is Parameter2.

   When the mf= value is null (mf=<>) or missing (nd= tag present) the matching
   algorithm is not used.

1. To check that a From header field is Authentic, the domain from the
   addr-spec in the From header field will be matched with the Signing
   Domain of the relevant DKIM2-Signature header field (see {{authentic-from}}).

   The value from the From header field is Parameter1 for the algorithm,
   the d= value is Parameter2.

1. When checking that a DSN (see {{bounce}}) has not been forged, a
   domain from the rt= value of the highest numbered DKIM2-Signature
   in the original message returned in the
   DSN needs to match with the Signing Domain (d=) used by the sender
   of the DSN (which will be found in the DKIM2-Signature field with i=1).

   The (decoded) value from rt= is Parameter1 to the algorithm, the d=
   value is Parameter2.

## Details of the relaxed domain match algorithm

The relaxed domain match algorithm proceeds as follows:

1. If one of the values being compared is an rt= value then the algorithm
   is operated for each email address within the (decoded) rt= value in
   turn until one matches or all fail to match.

1. If one of the values being compared is an email address then only the
   domain part of the value is used for the matches. The local
   part, any comments, mail parameters (along with the @ and any angle
   brackets) are ignored. A domain literal (e.g. `<x@[192.0.2.1]>`)
   never matches.

1. If there is an exact (caseless) match between Parameter1 and
   Parameter2 then the algorithm exits with a successful match result.

1. Otherwise, if Parameter1 contains no dot there is no match.

1. Otherwise, remove the leftmost label (the characters up to and
   including the first dot) from Parameter1 and return to step 3.

# Tag=Value Lists {#tagvaluelists}

DKIM2 uses a simple "tag=value" syntax in the Message-Instance and
DKIM2-Signature header fields, as well as in domain signature records
(see [DKIMKEYS]).

Values are a series of strings containing either text or a
base64string (see {{common-ABNF}}). The name of the tag will determine
the encoding and structure of each value.

Formally, the ABNF syntax rules are as follows:

    tag-list  =  tag-spec *( ";" tag-spec ) [ ";" ]
    tag-spec  =  [FWS] tag-name [FWS] "=" [FWS] tag-value [FWS]
    tag-name  =  ALPHA *(ALPHA / DIGIT / "_")
    tval      =  %x21-3A / %x3C-7E
                      ; not SEMICOLON
    tag-value =  [ tval *( 1*(WSP / FWS) tval ) ]
                      ; Prohibits WSP and FWS at beginning and end

Note that WSP is allowed anywhere around tags.  In particular, any
WSP after the "=" and any WSP before the terminating ";" is not part
of the value; however, WSP inside the value is significant.
Semicolon (";") characters MUST NOT occur in the tag value, since
that separates tag-specs. The semicolon at the end of the tag-list
is optional.

Each tag-spec MUST match one of the ABNF rules provided when a
tag-spec is being used in a specification. However, to allow
specifications to be updated, a tag-spec that is not recognized
is ignored.

A specification that uses a Tag=Value list will make it clear whether
tag-names or tag-values are case-sensitive or not. Absent any statement
to the contrary both items are case-sensitive. The specification
may also prescribe that whitespace within a tag-value is to be ignored.

Tags may appear in any order. Tags with duplicate names MUST NOT occur
within a single tag-list; if a tag name does occur more than once, the
entire tag-list is invalid.

Tags that have an empty value are not the same as omitted tags. An
empty value explicitly designates the empty string as the value. An
omitted tag is treated as having the default value, should one be
defined for that particular tag-spec. Tag=value pairs that represent
the default value MAY be included to aid legibility.

# The Message-Instance header field {#hfMessageInstance}

A Message-Instance header field documents the current contents of
the message and, in the case of a Reviser, records any relevant
changes that have been made to the incoming message.

The Message-Instance header field is a Tag=Value list (see
{{tagvaluelists}}). No tags have default values and all tag-names
MUST be in lower case.

The m= and h= tags MUST be present. The r= tag is optional.

ABNF:

    mi-field    = "Message-Instance:" tag-list

## m= the revision number of the Message-Instance header field

The Originator of a message uses the value 1. Further Message-Instance
header fields are added with a value one more than the current highest
numbered Message-Instance header field. A message with gaps in the
numbering is not a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message.

ABNF:

    mi-m-tag    = %s"m" [FWS] "=" [FWS] 1*DIGIT

## r= Recipes to recreate the previous instance of the message

The r= tag value is the base64 encoded version of the JSON object that
contains the Recipes that allow the previous instance of the message
to be recreated (see {{JSONrecipe}}).

ABNF:

    mi-r-tag    = %s"r" [FWS] "=" base64string

## h= the hash values for the message

The h= tag value contains the hash name, header hash value and body
hash value. Calculating the hash values is explained in {{messagehashes}}.

To provide for algorithmic dexterity more than one pair of hash values,
using a different algorithm MAY be supplied in the same Message-Instance
header field. An algorithm MUST NOT be present more than once
in a single Message-Instance header field; if the hash values were the
same this would be pointless and if they differ then the Signer is
presumably attempting to confuse a Verifier.

ABNF:

    mi-h-tag    = %s"h" [FWS] "=" hash-set *("," hash-set)
    hash-set    = [FWS] hash-name [FWS] ":" header-hash ":" body-hash
    hash-name   = %s"sha256" / %s"sha512" / x-hash-name
    header-hash = base64string
    body-hash   = base64string
    x-hash-name = textstring ; for later expansion

# The DKIM2-Signature header field {#hfDKIM2signature}

The signature of the email is stored in a DKIM2-Signature header
field. This header field is a Tag=Value list (see
{{tagvaluelists}}). No tags have default values and all tag-names
MUST be in lower case.

The i=, m=, t=, d= and s= tags MUST be present. There MUST be
either an nd= tag or both mf= and rt= tags. The other tags are optional.

ABNF:

    sig-field    = "DKIM2-Signature:" tag-list

It will be noted that we have not included a version number.  Experience
from IMF onward shows that it is essentially impossible to change
version numbers. If it becomes necessary to change DKIM2 in the sort
of incompatible way that a v=2 / v=3 version number would support,
it is expected that header fields will be labeled as DKIM3 instead.

## i= the sequence number of the DKIM2-Signature header field

The Originator of a message uses the value 1. Further DKIM2-Signature
header fields are added with a value one more than the current highest
numbered DKIM2-Signature header field. A message with gaps in the
numbering, other than below a DKIM2-Signature header field with the
"break" flag, is not a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message.

ABNF:

    sig-i-tag = %s"i" [FWS] "=" [FWS] 1*DIGIT

## m= the highest numbered Message-Instance header field

This value allows Verifiers to determine which entity made a particular
revision to the message header fields or body. The value specifies the
highest numbered Message-Instance header field in the message at the
point at which this DKIM2-Signature header field was added.

ABNF:

    sig-m-tag = %s"m" [FWS] "=" [FWS] 1*DIGIT

## n= nonce value

This text value, if present, is for the creator of the DKIM2-Signature
header field to use for any purpose they wish. For example, it can be
used as an index into a database to assist in handling Delivery Status
Notifications received after delivery failures.

To discourage use of this tag field as an alternative to the use of more
appropriate header fields, the length of the string is restricted
to 64 characters.

Note the value MUST be simple ASCII and MUST NOT contain semicolon.

ABNF:

    sig-n-tag   = %s"n" [FWS] "=" [FWS] nonce-value
    nonce-value = *64(%x21-3A / %x3C-7E)
                      ; printable ASCII except semicolon, max 64 chars

## t= signature timestamp

The time that this header field was created. The format is the number of
seconds since 00:00:00 on January 1, 1970 in the UTC time zone.  The value
is expressed as an unsigned integer in decimal ASCII.  This value
is not constrained to fit into a 31- or 32-bit integer.

It is expected that values up to at least 10^12 will occur (this fits
into 40 bits and will cover messages sent until approximately 33,700 AD).

ABNF:

    sig-t-tag    = %s"t" [FWS] "=" [FWS] 1*DIGIT

## mf= the MAIL FROM used when the message was sent

DKIM2 records the [RFC5321] MAIL FROM value that was used when the message
was transmitted over an SMTP link from the signing MTA. Note that MAIL FROM
may be just "<>", for example for a Delivery Status Notification.

The value is recorded as the base64 encoding of the [RFC5321] reverse-path
because of the complex syntax of reverse-path values (which can include
characters which would confuse naive parsers of DKIM2-Signature header
fields). The angle brackets MUST be included, but any "Mail-parameters"
that were present after the reverse-path MUST NOT be included.

ABNF:

    sig-mf-tag  = %s"mf" [FWS] "=" base64string

## rt= the RCPT TO value(s) used when the message was sent

DKIM2 records the [RFC5321] RCPT TO value(s) that were used when the message
was transmitted over an SMTP link from the signing MTA.

The value is recorded as the base64 encoding of the [RFC5321] Forward-path
because of the complex syntax of Forward-path values (which can include
characters which would confuse naive parsers of DKIM2-Signature header
fields). The angle brackets MUST be included, but any "Rcpt-parameters"
that were present after the Forward-path MUST NOT be included.

When a message is intended for more than one recipient then the rt= tag
MAY include all of the recipients so that a single copy of the email
can be sent to all of the recipients in one SMTP transaction.
Alternatively, multiple copies of the email MAY be generated, each
recording only the RCPT TO values used for that copy.

However, an address given in a "Bcc:" header field MUST NOT appear in
the rt= tag of any copy of the message delivered to a recipient
that is not a bcc recipient (see [RFC5322] Section 3.6.3).

ABNF:

    sig-rt-tag = %s"rt" [FWS] "=" base64string *("," base64string)

## nd= the domain that will appear in the next DKIM2-Signature header field

This tag is used in order to provide a valid Chain of Custody when a
message is being forwarded from a different domain than the one to
which it arrived. The domain MUST be a valid DNS name under which a
DKIM2 key record is published.

The domain name in the nd= tag MUST exactly match the domain name of the
d= tag of the next DKIM2-Signature in sequence (i=) order.

Note that when an nd= tag is present both mf= and rt= MUST be omitted.
It is permissible to have a sequence of more than one
DKIM2-Signature header fields with an nd= tag, but this sequence
(in unbroken ascending numeric order of i= tags) MUST end with a
DKIM2-Signature that contains mf= and rt= tags.

ABNF:

    sig-nd-tag   = %s"nd" [FWS] "=" [FWS] Domain

## d= the Signing Domain associated with this signature

This domain is used to form the query for the public key. The domain
MUST be a valid DNS name under which the DKIM2 key record is published.

The domain name in the d= tag MUST exactly match the rightmost labels of
the domain name of the mf= tag. That is to say, the domain name of the
mf= tag MUST either match the d= domain exactly or be a sub-domain
of the d= domain name.

When the mf= domain is empty (it decodes to "<>"), as will be the case
for Delivery Status Notifications (DSNs), then no match is required.

ABNF:

    sig-d-tag   = %s"d" [FWS] "=" [FWS] Domain

## s= the signature value(s) for the message

The s= tag value contains the Selector, signature algorithm name and
signature value. Calculating the value is explained in
{{calculate-signature}} and the algorithms are described in
{{algorithms}}.

The Selector values subdivide the namespace for the domain being
used for signing. Note that the DNS lookup for the public key
will check that the k= algorithm value is a match for the
algorithm supplied in the tag.

To provide for algorithmic dexterity more than one signature,
using different algorithms, MAY be supplied. One additional signature
using the same algorithm (perhaps with a different key length) MAY be
present provided that a different Selector is used.

A Selector MUST NOT be present more than once in a single DKIM2-Signature
header field; if the signature values were the same this would be
pointless and if they differ then the Signer is presumably attempting
to confuse a Verifier.

ABNF:

    sig-s-tag   = %s"s" [FWS] "=" [FWS] sig-set *( "," sig-set )
    sig-set     = selector [FWS] ":" [FWS] sig-name [FWS] ":" message-sig
    sig-name    = %s"rsa-sha256" / %s"ed25519-sha256" /
                  %s"mldsa44-ed25519-sha512" / x-sig-name
    x-sig-name  = textstring     ; for later extension
    message-sig = base64string

## f= flags

Flags serve two purposes; they either report what has been done to
the message by the system creating the DKIM2-Signature or they make
a request to systems that handle the mail thereafter. Flags are
separated by commas, and optional white-space allows systems to
add several flags without creating long lines.

If a flag value is not recognized it has no effect.

The flag values that report things are:

"exploded": this message is being sent to more than one email address.
This flag is of importance when detecting DKIM Replay attacks
(see {{DKIM-Replay}}).

"break": the message reached this Signer with DKIM2 header fields that
did not permit the chain to be continued, and this Signer has started
a new Chain of Custody without removing them (see {{verification}}).

The flag values that make requests are:

"feedback": this Signer requests feedback about how this message is handled
during delivery and thereafter. This document does not describe what such
feedback might be or where it might be delivered. If this flag is absent
then feedback is explicitly not required.

"feedhere": this Signer requests that any feedback about how this message is
handled during delivery and thereafter is relayed via this hop. This flag
will be set by privacy-conscious Forwarders when a message has a "feedback"
flag set and the Forwarder does not wish the identity of systems to which
the message is forwarded to be revealed to the requestor of feedback.

ABNF:

    sig-f-tag        = %s"f" [FWS] "=" [FWS] sig-f-tag-data
                       *( [FWS] "," [FWS] sig-f-tag-data)
    sig-f-tag-data   = %s"feedback" / %s"feedhere" / %s"exploded" /
                       %s"break" / x-sig-f-tag-data
    x-sig-f-tag-data = textstring ; for later extension

# From header fields are required to be Authentic {#authentic-from}

Email addresses that appear in From header fields ([RFC5322]) will be
displayed by MUAs to users. It needs it to be clear that the use of that
email address was authorized -- this specification expresses this as
a requirement that the From header field is "Authentic".

A From header field is Authentic when there is a relaxed domain match
(see {{relaxed-domain-match}}) between the email address in the
From header field and the Signing Domain found in the d= tag
of the relevant DKIM2-Signature header field. The Authentic
property is demonstrated by the ability to use the domain to sign.

When the From header field has not been altered since the message
was signed for the very first time, i.e. when the i=1
DKIM2-Signature field was created, then that From and that
DKIM2-Signature are the relevant header fields for the comparison.

If another system replaces the From header field, which will be
apparent by inspecting the header Recipes (see {{header-recipes}}),
then the lowest numbered (i=) DKIM2-Signature header field whose
m= value is greater than or equal to the m= value of the
Message-Instance header field with the Recipe will,
along with the new From header field, be the relevant header
fields for comparison.

A From header field with more than one email address, or one that
uses domain-literals (IP addresses instead of domains), can
never be Authentic.

# Signer actions

This section gives the actions that need to be undertaken by the Signer
of a message. They may be done in any appropriate order.

If a message is not the initial form of a message (that is, it
already contains DKIM2-Signature or Message-Instance header
fields) then a Signer MUST NOT sign the message unless it is
a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message (see {{verification}}) or would be one
but for its From header field not being Authentic, the Signer sets
the "break" flag (see {{verification}}), or
there is an out-of-band arrangement covering the situation
(see {{imaginary-hops}}).

## Add any necessary Message-Instance header fields

If a system is generating the initial form of a message or if
it is a Reviser that has made changes to the message body and/or
header fields then it MUST compute the body hash as described in
{{computing-body-hash}} and the hash of the header fields
as described in {{computing-header-hash}}.

If the message does not contain a Message-Instance header field then one
MUST be added. If hashing the message body or relevant header fields does not
give the same hash values as those recorded in the highest version
(m=) Message-Instance header field then a new Message-Instance
header field MUST be added.

Such a Message-Instance header field MUST contain Recipes (unless
the "break" flag is set, see {{verification}}) to allow
recreation of the message corresponding to the hash values in the
previously highest numbered Message-Instance header field.
Alternatively, it could contain a
null Recipe to indicate that recreating the previous version
of the message body will not be possible. Null Recipes cannot
be used in respect of header field changes.

A system may add more than one Message-Instance header field if it
wishes to do so, but the DKIM2 design allows all modifications made by
any single system to be documented
in a single Message-Instance header field.

Note that the first (m=1) Message-Instance header field MAY
contain Recipes if it is wished to record any changes made to a
message as it enters the DKIM2 ecosystem.

Adding a Message-Instance field with no Recipes (and thus
the same hash values as the previously highest numbered
Message-Instance field) is not forbidden; however, it is most
likely to be pointless and a waste of time and energy for all concerned.

## Provide a Chain of Custody for the message {#chain-of-custody}

The DKIM2-Signature header field contains the MAIL FROM
and RCPT TO values that will be used when the message is transmitted,
so these [RFC5321] "envelope" values MUST be available to (or
deducible by) a Signer.

The receiver of a message will check for an exact match (including
the local parts of the email addresses) between the MAIL FROM / RCPT TO
[RFC5321] protocol values and the mf= and rt= values in the highest numbered
(most recent) DKIM2-Signature header field. The matching will be case
sensitive for the local part (before the @) and case insensitive for
the domain (after the @).

It is acceptable for there to
be more RCPT TO email addresses recorded in rt= than are actually used in
the SMTP conversation, but any RCPT TO value which is used MUST be present
in the rt= tag.

Verifiers will check for a relaxed domain match (see {{relaxed-domain-match}})
between the Signing Domain (d=) and the domain in the MAIL FROM value.

When the message being signed already has a DKIM2-Signature header field
(i.e. it has already been transmitted at least once) then the Signer MUST
ensure that the message has a valid Chain of Custody. That is,
a relaxed domain match (see {{relaxed-domain-match}}) is present between the
mf= value used and at least one of the
rt= values on the highest numbered DKIM2-Signature header field of the
existing message. This Chain of Custody contributes to the way in
which DKIM2 tackles DKIM Replay attacks.

It may be that an MTA does not change anything in the message which would require
a new Message-Instance header field and, additionally, it could forward it in
such a manner that a Verifier would be able to verify the existing message
(that is to say the MAIL FROM and RCPT TO will have appropriate values). In
such a case, the MTA could choose not to add a DKIM2-Signature header field.
To put this another way, an essentially transparent SMTP forwarding system
need not be made "DKIM2 aware".

## Chain of Custody for imaginary hops {#imaginary-hops}

If a message is being forwarded then the MAIL FROM on the outgoing
message (and the mf= tag which records this value) MUST be set in
such a way that the Chain of Custody is not broken. However, it may
be that the domains used for the delivery of the incoming message
and the domains used for sending the message onward are not the same.

For example, the incoming email might have been addressed to
example.com, but the outgoing email will be sent from example.net.

One way of ensuring that the Chain of Custody remains valid
would be to generate an extra DKIM2-Signature
header field that causes values to match, i.e. a record is fabricated
that documents the mail being passed from one domain to another with
appropriate "mf=" and "rt=" tag values which document the imaginary
transfer of the email from one domain to the next.

Alternatively the "nd=" tag may be used. When present the next higher
sequence numbered DKIM2-Signature header field MUST be signed by the
specified domain. However, since there are no mf= or rt= tag values
there is no need to provide imaginary values for the imaginary "hop"
between the domain used for receiving the message and the domain used to
forward it.

It will be noted that the creation of an extra DKIM2-Signature header field
(whether the nd= scheme is used or not) will require the Signer to have access
to a DKIM2 private key associated with a domain that is a relaxed domain
match for the domain in the RCPT TO entry of the message as it arrived.

Access to this private key is
often achieved by the Signer creating the private key and never sharing it.
One of two approaches is then taken to publishing the public key.
The first is to provide the public key (and Selector value) to the domain owner
who creates an appropriate DNS entry. The alternative is for the Signer
to create a public
key DNS entry within a part of the DNS that they control and the domain owner
publishes a CNAME pointing at this.

The general expectation is that signatures with an nd= tag have been
applied by the same system as the next DKIM2-Signature field
without an nd= tag. However, as a special case (where arrangements
have been put in place to cover the situation) a message with an
nd= tag in the highest numbered DKIM2-Signature header field may
be sent over an SMTP connection. The receiving system will not be able
to validate the MAIL FROM and RCPT TO values, so the message will not
be a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message; whether it is acceptable will depend
on out-of-band arrangements. The system MUST then provide a DKIM2-Signature
header field with a d= tag that exactly matches the nd= tag. Adding
such a header field will signal that the break in the Chain of Custody
is intentional.

## Ensure that From header fields are Authentic

The Signing Domain to be used has to be consistent with the
requirements already outlined for creating a valid Chain of Custody.

Furthermore, if the original message is being signed, or the From
header field has been modified, the Signing Domain MUST be chosen so
that the From header field in the message will be Authentic
(see {{authentic-from}}).

In simple terms, the Signing Domain will be equal to, or a parent
of, both the MAIL FROM domain and the From header field domain
for the original message and whenever the From header field is
modified by an intermediary.

## Determine if an "exploded" flag is required

An intermediary that receives a message and then sends copies of the
message to more than one email address is said to have "exploded" it.
In such a case the exploded flag MUST be set in the DKIM2-Signature header
field that is being created to sign the outgoing messages. The flag
MUST be set even if the outgoing messages differ.

The flag MUST NOT be set on the very first DKIM2-Signature header
field (i=1).

The purpose of the flag is to give receivers information that
could assist in the detection of DKIM Replay attacks (see {{DKIM-Replay}}).

## Record an accurate timestamp

Verifiers check the timestamps in DKIM2-Signature header fields and
if the message is "too old" or "from the future" will classify the
message as not being a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message. These checks
are made to allow systems to safely discard cached information
that they may be using to handle DSNs or to detect DKIM Replay.

Hence, a Signer MUST provide an accurate timestamp (t=) value, albeit
Verifiers only check it against the limits set out in {{check-timestamps}}.

## Determine which Selector value(s) to use {#signer_privatekey}

The Signer has to record Selector value(s) in the DKIM2-Signature
header field. Those Selector values allow a Verifier to fetch
public key values. The Signer will need to have access to the
corresponding private key to calculate the signature(s). How
this is achieved is not a matter for this specification.

## Calculate a signature value {#calculate-signature}

A Signer calculates a signature solely over the Message-Instance and
DKIM2-Signature header fields of the message. The hashes of
the body and other header fields are covered by the hashes in
the highest version (m=) Message-Instance header field and hence
the signature will in practice be signing the message as a whole.

Most cryptographic schemes proceed by first calculating a hash value
and then signing the hash value, but the DKIM2-Signature header field
only provides the final signature value. This means that there
is no difficulty if the hash value is inordinately long, or is
not emitted by the cryptographic routine being used.

The Signer MUST apply the following steps
in the order given (which are not quite the same as the steps
undertaken in calculating header hashes).

*  Convert all relevant header field names (not the header field values) to
   lowercase.  For example, convert "DKIM2-signature" to "dkim2-signature".

*  Unfold all header field continuation lines as described in
   [RFC5322]; in particular, lines with terminators embedded in
   continued header field values (that is, CRLF sequences followed by
   WSP) MUST be interpreted without the CRLF.  Implementations MUST
   NOT remove the CRLF at the end of the header field value.

*  Delete all WSP characters. This means all WSP characters before and
   after the colon separating the header field name from the header
   field value, all WSP characters within the unfolded header field
   value and all trailing WSP characters before the CRLF. The colon
   separator and the CRLF MUST be retained.

*  Place the header fields in order. First come the Message-Instance
   header fields in ascending instance (m=) order. Second are the
   DKIM2-Signature header fields in ascending sequence (i=) order.
   Last of all is an incomplete DKIM2-Signature header field (the
   one that this system is creating) with all tags present except
   that the signature value(s) within the (s=) value are set to
   the null string (""). The incomplete header field MUST be
   unfolded, MUST have a trailing CRLF and MUST have WSP removed
   in just the same way as the
   complete header fields being processed.

*  The concatenated header fields are then fed to the signature
   algorithm(s). Once all the values are available the null
   signature value strings
   are replaced by the base64 values of the signatures.

# Verification {#verification}

A Verifier determines whether or not a message is a Validly
Signed DKIM2 Message. That is whether or not the message meets all the
requirements set out in this specification on Signers, particularly
those for generating DKIM2-Signature and Message-Instance header
fields. Further, when a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message is transmitted
over SMTP, the MAIL FROM and RCPT TO values need to be consistent
with values in the highest numbered DKIM2-Signature header field.

The specific checks that a Verifier makes and a sensible order in
which to make them are discussed in {{verifier_actions}}.
This section considers how a Verifier fits into the DKIM2 ecosystem.

It is entirely a matter for local policy whether a message being
a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message makes any difference to whether
or not a message is accepted for delivery.

However, if the message contains DKIM2-Signature or
Message-Instance header fields but it is not a Validly Signed
DKIM2 Message then the existing chain MUST NOT be continued and the
message sent to another system unless the "break" flag is set as
described below, or some local arrangement is in place. A message that
fails only because its From header field is not Authentic can be
continued without the "break" flag. The reason for this is that
the receiving system will be able to determine that
the message is not a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message and that this
would have been entirely apparent at the previous hop.

To put it another way, it is not possible for an intermediary
to "patch up" an invalid message to make it valid, so an attempt
to forward the message, without prior arrangement, could well
fail and adverse conclusions could be drawn from that failure.

Of course, discarding all the DKIM2 header fields and starting
again (at i=1 m=1) will mean that whatever the previous
history of the message might be, there will be a new
Chain of Custody starting at this point. Naturally, attention
will need to be paid to the provision that From header fields
are required to be Authentic (see {{authentic-from}}).

Alternatively, the Signer can keep the existing DKIM2 header fields
and set the "break" flag in the DKIM2-Signature header field that it
adds. It MUST also add a new Message-Instance header field, which need
not contain Recipes, and MUST first remove any DKIM2-Signature or
Message-Instance header field whose i= or m= value cannot be
determined or is duplicated, so that the header fields can be placed
in order (see {{calculate-signature}}). The Signer is then the
Originator of the message: Verifiers treat its DKIM2-Signature header
field and that Message-Instance header field as the i=1 and m=1 header
fields, stop verifying there, and MUST NOT rely on any lower numbered
DKIM2-Signature or Message-Instance header field for any purpose.
Those header fields remain, covered by the new signature, as a record
of the message as the Signer received it.

## Provisionally Verified messages

In order to allow receivers of messages to operate efficiently,
they MAY make an initial assessment of the likelihood that the
message is a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message and accept delivery.

If the receiver then determines, upon closer inspection that the
message is not a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message after all, or they
have some other reason for not wishing to accept the message --
perhaps based on analysis of its contents -- then they MAY
generate a Delivery Status Notification (DSN) and send that
back to the system that delivered the message to them.
The details of this procedure appear in {{bounce}}.

This initial assessment is achieved by a Verifier ascertaining
that the signature in the highest numbered (i=) DKIM2-Signature
header field is correct and that the hashes in the highest
numbered (m=) Message-Instance header field match
the message to hand. If so, then the message is said to be
Provisionally Verified.

If the checks fail and the message cannot be Provisionally
Verified but, nevertheless, the message is accepted and delivered
then a DSN for the email MUST NOT be generated thereafter.
Hence, the best strategy for email that is not wanted or is
clearly invalidly signed, is to reject it (with a 5xx error
code) while the relevant SMTP conversation is still ongoing.
It will then be for the sending system to determine what happens
thereafter.

If a Verifier gives a TEMPERROR result (it is not possible with
the information to hand to determine whether or not the email
is a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message, see {{output-states}}) a 4xx
error code SHOULD be used in the SMTP conversation to allow
the sending MTA to understand the situation. If the email passed
Provisional Verification then further attempts to obtain the
missing information SHOULD be made. If these fail the TEMPERROR
might usefully be upgraded to a PERMERROR before being recorded
in any DSN that is sent.

The mechanism set out in {{imaginary-hops}} envisages out-of-band
arrangements to allow the sending of messages with the highest
numbered DKIM2-Signature header field containing an nd= tag. If no
such arrangements are in place then the message is not Provisionally
Verified. If the arrangements are in
place and later analysis shows that the message cannot be
converted into a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message then further
out-of-band arrangements will be needed to deal with reporting
this (using a Delivery Status Notification message or otherwise)
because the provisions in this specification will not be adequate.

## SMTP return codes

Any 4xx or 5xx return code is sufficient to signal
that an email cannot currently be delivered and whether or
not a retry could succeed.

However, if an MTA wishes to specifically signal that it
is rejecting a message where signatures are missing
or do not verify, a 550/5.7.x reply code SHOULD be used.

If an MTA wishes to explicitly signal that it has not been
possible to fetch a public key because of an apparently
temporary DNS issue then a 451/4.7.5 reply code SHOULD be used.

Enhanced status codes are defined in [RFC3463], including
X.7.5 (cryptographic failure). [RFC7372] adds codes for email
authentication failures, such as 5.7.20 (no passing DKIM
signature found).

## Further observations on verification

If the message has been modified since its original creation then
the Message-Instance header fields will usually enable a Verifier to
"undo" those modifications and reconstruct each preceding version
of the message.

In particular, a receiving system may wish to assess the original form
of the message. All Recipes recorded in Message-Instance header
fields could be undone in order (provided
no null Recipe is present) and an assessment then made of whether the
original message was a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message and in particular
whether the From header field is Authentic.

This assessment of the initial form of the message can be done
without considering any other signatures and hash values
except those on the i=1 and m=1 header fields and the simplicity
of this check may be useful to some receivers.

However, determining that the original message was valid is only
one part of determining whether the message being delivered is a
Validly Signed DKIM2 Message; other checks will need to be made.

# Verifier actions {#verifier_actions}

This section discusses the detail of the actions taken by a
Verifier to determine whether or not a message is a Validly Signed
DKIM2 Message.

The hash values for the header fields and the body are recalculated
and compared with those in the Message-Instance header field. The
hash of the Message-Instance and DKIM2-Signature header fields is
then calculated (with signature values removed) and the signature
is checked using the Signer's public key.

The details of doing the hash calculations involve repeating all
the actions taken by a Signer and to avoid a lot of repetition
those actions are not spelled out in any detail.

In order for a Verifier to determine whether a particular DKIM2-Signature
header field is valid it will have to reconstruct the state of the
message when that header field was added. That will require
considering all the Recipes that were recorded in the message after
the signature was calculated.

## Output states {#output-states}

For compatibility with the Authentication-Results header field defined
in [RFC8601] a verification will result in one of four states.

The states report whether or not the message has traveled to the
destination without being modified or sent along a delivery path
other than one consistent with this specification. They do not attest
in any way as to whether the message will be of value to the
recipient or whether any modifications that have been made are or
are not benign.

The four states are:

PASS:  The message was successfully verified.

FAIL:  The message could be verified but a hash or signature was not
       correct.

PERMERROR:  The message could not be verified due to some error that
      is unrecoverable, such as a required header field being absent
      or malformed.

TEMPERROR:  The message could not be verified due to a temporary
      inability to retrieve a public key. A later attempt may
      produce a different result.

A Verifier MAY cease verifying once a single failure is detected.

Verifiers wishing to communicate the results of verification to other
parts of the mail system may do so in whatever manner they see fit. If
they wish to provide a human-readable string to describe a failure
to verify (any state except PASS) then in order to provide the
maximum possible assistance to senders they SHOULD use the text
strings specified in this document. These human-readable messages
are described with m=`<x>` or tag=`<y>` placeholders;
the `<x>` and `<y>` MUST be replaced with the relevant ordinal
or tag name (without the `<` and `>` characters). Similarly
`<value>` MUST be replaced by a relevant string for the particular
message; and `<s1>` and `<s2>` MUST be replaced by appropriate Selector
names.

If the verification is being performed during an SMTP protocol
conversation the human-readable string SHOULD be part of the
5xx or 4xx response string.

If the results of the verification are being communicated in a
Delivery Status Notification message ([RFC6522]) the
human-readable string SHOULD be included.

If a system wishes to record the results returned by a Verifier
into a message then the Authentication-Results header field ([RFC8601])
is available for this purpose. DKIM2 specific values
are defined in [AUTHRESULTS]. It should be noted that this header
field is ignored when calculating header hash values.

If verification results are recorded then that record SHOULD
include the human-readable string.

## Ensure that the DKIM2 header fields are valid

Verifiers will need to meticulously validate the format and values of all
relevant Message-Instance and DKIM2-Signature header fields. They
will also need to ensure that all required instances of these header
fields are present and that all required tags are present and tags that
cannot appear together do not do so. Unknown tags are to be ignored
as set out in {{tagvaluelists}}.

No Validly Signed DKIM2 Message will contain a Message-Instance field
with a higher m= value than occurs in any DKIM2-Signature field.

There are also requirements on not repeating hash algorithms or
Selectors or providing the same signing algorithm more than twice.

To assist debugging, errors in a JSON object specifying Recipes
SHOULD be called out specifically. If the JSON is valid but the
Verifier is not prepared to handle the complexity of the Recipes
then a failure MAY be returned but the error message SHOULD NOT
confuse this situation with invalid JSON. What "unacceptably
complex" might mean is a local policy decision.

Clearly, a message with no DKIM2 header fields at all will be
reported as PERMERROR.

Possible errors:

    PERMERROR: Message-Instance m=<x> missing
    PERMERROR: Message-Instance m=<x> syntax error
    PERMERROR: Message-Instance m=<x> tag=<y> missing
    PERMERROR: Message-Instance m=<x> is not signed
    PERMERROR: Message-Instance m=<x> has a duplicate hash algorithm
    PERMERROR: Message-Instance m=<x> contains invalid JSON
    PERMERROR: Message-Instance m=<x> JSON is unacceptably complex
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> missing
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> syntax error
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> tag=<y> missing
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> tag=<y> was unexpected
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> has a duplicate selector
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> has more selectors than allowed
    PERMERROR: No DKIM2 header fields found

## Check the timestamps {#check-timestamps}

A message is not a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message if any of its
DKIM2-Signature header fields was created more than 14 days
before the message was delivered.

This limit is intended to assist in the design of schemes
that attempt to detect DKIM Replay without impacting transmission
of messages on today's highly reliable and highly connected Internet.

It might be expected that the timestamps on the DKIM2-Signature
fields were in an ascending order. However, many systems do not
always keep their clocks tightly synchronized with reality, and
interworking will be impacted if sub-second accuracy is required
of MTAs. That said, a timestamp that comes from the far future
may be an attempt to confuse DKIM Replay systems. Hence a message
is not a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message if any timestamp is from
more than one day ahead of when the message was delivered.

It should be noted that checking timestamps requires accurate
information about when a message was delivered and a Verifier
cannot assess the message otherwise.

Possible errors:

    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> signature expired
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> signature is from the future

## Check the Chain of Custody

As explained in {{chain-of-custody}} to check for a valid Chain
of Custody a Verifier will need to check for an exact
match between the MAIL FROM and RCPT TO parameters used when delivering
a message and the values found in the mf= and rt= tags of the highest
numbered DKIM2-Signature header field. There may be extra values
in the rt= value, but all RCPT TO values actually used for
delivery will need to be present.

When comparing with SMTP protocol values the local part of the email
address is checked as well as the domain. The domains are compared
without regard to case and need to match exactly. As is usual in email
protocols the case of the local part of an email address is assumed
to matter (and see also {{EAI}}), so the local parts need to match
exactly. Note that the decoded mf= and rt= values used for these
matches include the angle brackets around the email addresses.

A Verifier will also need to check, for every DKIM2-Signature field,
that there is a relaxed domain match (see {{relaxed-domain-match}})
between the d= Signing Domain and the mf= value. However, if the
(decoded) mf= value is null (mf=<>) or mf= is absent (there is an
nd= tag present) then no match is performed.

A Verifier then needs to check the Chain of Custody from start
to finish. The steps for this are set out in {{rdm-uses}}
Item [](#cc-rdm){: format="counter"} and are not repeated here.

If out-of-band arrangements (as explained in {{imaginary-hops}}) apply
then there may not be a "next DKIM2-Signature" in the message -- it
is about to be added. Hence, although the message is not presently
a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message, the intention is that the receiving
system will add a DKIM2-Signature header field whose d= matches the
nd= value, to fix this.

Possible errors:

    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> MAIL FROM <value> did not match
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> RCPT TO <value> did not match
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> MAIL FROM and d= do not match
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> nd= does not match
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> unexpected nd= tag
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> mf= does not match the rt= of i=<y>
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> d= does not match the rt= of i=<y>

## Fetch the public key

The public keys of the signatures in DKIM2-Signature fields are
needed to complete the verification process. Details of key management and
representation are described in {{key_management}} and [DKIMKEYS].
A key record that is malformed cannot be used and the result
is a PERMERROR.

Note that DNS timeouts MUST be reported as TEMPERROR but a DNS
result that indicates the key is absent MUST be reported as a
PERMERROR. Additionally, as [DKIMKEYS] makes clear, if more than
one record is returned this is an error. The human-readable error
message SHOULD provide the Selector value so that it is clear which
key has caused a problem.

Note that [DKIMKEYS] has retired the h= field and DKIM2 takes no
notice of it should it be present.

Possible errors:

    TEMPERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> public key <s1> could not be fetched
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> public key <s1> does not exist
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> public key <s1> has multiple records
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> public key <s1> has a syntax error
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> public key <s1> algorithm mismatch
    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> public key <s1> has been revoked

## Perform the signature verification calculation

Verifying a signature consists of actions semantically equivalent to the
following steps:

1.  Prepare a canonicalized version of the Message-Instance and DKIM2-Signature
    header fields as described in {{calculate-signature}}. The signature value(s)
    themselves will need to be removed to correspond with what was actually
    signed. Note that this canonicalized version does not actually replace
    the original content.

1.  Use the relevant public key value(s) to check the signature(s).

1.  If there is more than one signature provided then they MUST all be
    checked if the Verifier is able to do so. If any signature fails then
    an error SHOULD be reported. If all signatures that can be checked fail
    then FAIL MUST be reported.

1.  If some signatures fail and others pass then any error that is
    reported SHOULD provide that information by specifying the relevant
    Selector values.

The reasoning for reporting an error if any signature fails is that if a signature
scheme has recently become deprecated because it is known to be cryptographically
flawed then Signers will use a second (unbroken) signature scheme. However, such
a Signer may still provide the other signature for the benefit of Verifiers
that have yet to upgrade -- reasoning perhaps that attacks are too expensive
to be a very significant security issue. A Verifier that determines that
one signature passes while the other fails may well be in a position to
prevent an attack.

Possible errors:

    FAIL: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> selector <s1> incorrect signature
    FAIL: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> signature <s1> signature <s2> failed

## Validating body and header hashes

Verifying a hash value requires a Verifier to repeat the hash calculation
performed by the Signer as set out in {{computing-header-hash}}
and {{computing-body-hash}}. The values can then be directly compared.

Since there may be more than one hash algorithm given, the human-readable
error message SHOULD indicate which algorithm's result failed to match.

If one hash value passes and another does not then this SHOULD be
treated as a failure.

Possible errors:

    FAIL: Message-Instance m=<x> header hash <value> mismatch
    FAIL: Message-Instance m=<x> body hash <value> mismatch

## Check for Authentic From header fields

The From header fields within a Validly Signed DKIM2 Message are
required to be Authentic.

A Verifier will need to inspect the currently present From header
field and any previous From header fields that Recipes show have
been present. To determine if the From header field is Authentic
the relaxed domain match comparison ({{relaxed-domain-match}}) is used
to compare the domain(s) in the From header field(s) with the
relevant DKIM2-Signature field(s).

Details of how to identify the relevant header fields are given
in {{authentic-from}}. Note that a From header field with more
than one address, or with a domain-literal, can never be Authentic.

Possible errors:

    PERMERROR: DKIM2-Signature i=<x> matching From is not authentic

# DKIM Replay attacks {#DKIM-Replay}

In a DKIM Replay attack a message with a valid signature but
containing malicious content is sent to large numbers of recipients.
The original Signer may only have signed one message but, without
their knowledge, the malicious message is "replayed" at scale by
a third party. However, since the signature on the message is valid,
reputation systems may allow the messages to be delivered.

Clearly, a Signer that wishes to preserve their good name will want
to avoid signing malicious content, but the reality is that identifying
that an individual message is malicious can be difficult.

Receivers hosting numerous mailboxes can expect to see large
numbers of identical messages sent to them directly from the
originating system. Mechanisms for dealing with DKIM Replay
need to be aware of this and act appropriately.

What distinguishes a DKIM Replay attack, where a third party forwards
signed messages, from an attack by a Signer that sends many malicious
messages itself is that the original Signer may have limited the
number of messages it allows to be sent, and the replay sidesteps
that limit.

## Preventing and detecting DKIM Replay attacks

In DKIM2 a number of mechanisms make DKIM Replay much more difficult
to achieve than has been the case with DKIM1. The Chain of Custody
ensures that any system that is replaying will have provided
cryptographic proof that they were an intermediary, and this
information can inform decisions about whether or not to accept
a message.

Systems that provide service for large numbers of mailboxes can
track whether the same message has been received more than once,
having passed via an intermediary, without an "exploded" flag
being added to indicate that multiple copies are
to be expected. A message will have passed via an intermediary
if there is more than one DKIM2-Signature header field present. A
message will be "the same" if the body hash
value of the original email (Message-Instance m=1) matches.

Alternatively, if an exploded flag is present this will pin down
where a system is claiming to have sent multiple copies. Assessing
whether the flag was expected can affect decisions about delivery.

If a system only learns the details of a single replayed message
then of course counting is of no value, but if the system
doing bulk replay has added an exploded flag then this can
still affect decisions regarding delivery.

A Receiver can attempt to distinguish a DKIM Replay attack from
bulk sending (if they view this as valuable) by considering whether
the emails arrived via an unexpected route, via a third party.

Receivers need to be aware that they may receive multiple copies
of the same message if different recipients of an original all
choose to forward the message to the receiver. Malicious DKIM
Replay attacks generally happen at such volume that distinguishing
this re-convergence case is unlikely to be especially complex.

It should be noted, as [RFC1047] explains, that
duplicated messages can result from a protocol level issue but, with
appropriately set timer values, duplication is rare on today's
Internet.

# Delivery Status Notifications in the DKIM2 ecosystem {#bounce}

In the DKIM2 ecosystem, when a message cannot be delivered then
this is reported to the sending machine by means of an [RFC5321]
return code or, if the SMTP session has completed, by generating
a Delivery Status Notification (DSN), as defined in [RFC6522].

A DSN is sent to the MAIL FROM address in the usual manner. This
address can be found in the mf= tag value (decoded from base64) of
the highest numbered DKIM2-Signature header field. If this value
has no address ("<>") then a DSN MUST NOT be sent.

DKIM2 requires that there is relaxed domain match between the
Signing Domain and the mf= value in the same DKIM2-Signature header
field. Hence, since a DSN will only be
sent when a message has been Provisionally Verified, there can be
no "backscatter" to the forged addresses of third parties that
have not been involved in handling the message.

DKIM2 expects failure reports (DSNs) to be passed back in turn to
each previous (validated) hop that was taken on the message's
outward journey until a system is reached that is prepared to
take full responsibility for the failure event. This method of
propagating DSNs gives MTAs that were involved in passing the
message forward the opportunity to adjust their future sending
behavior.

## DSN contents

As set out in [RFC6522], a DSN has a top-level MIME part of
type `multipart/report`.

A valid DSN generated by a DKIM2 system MUST always contain three
sections, the human-readable text, the machine readable status (in a
message/delivery-status MIME part) and either a MIME part of type
`message/rfc822` that holds the original message exactly as it was
submitted by the sending system or `text/rfc822-headers` which has
just the header fields of that message.

The message being reported upon MUST have been Provisionally
Verified.

The DSN itself MUST have appropriate Message-Instance and
DKIM2-Signature fields, noting that the MAIL FROM to be used
will be null ("<>").

## DSN propagation

A Forwarder which receives a DSN MAY propagate this DSN to the
MAIL FROM address used to deliver the message to it
(which can be found in the highest numbered DKIM2-Signature
header field). A Forwarder that set the "break" flag MUST NOT do so.

When propagating the DSN back along the forwarding chain
the first and second parts of the DSN MUST be rewritten so
as to remove any information about the destination to
which the message was sent.

Additionally, the original message in the third part of the
DSN MUST be converted back to the state the message was in
when it originally arrived -- before it was forwarded.

If the message was modified by the Forwarder on the outward
journey, those modifications MUST be undone and the Message-Instance
header field documenting those changes MUST be removed.

All DKIM2-Signature header fields added by this Forwarder
(including any with an nd= tag) MUST be removed. A good faith
effort SHOULD also be made to remove any header fields that are
not signed by DKIM2, such as Received header fields, that were
not present when the message arrived on its outward journey.

If it is impossible to regenerate the body of the message, because
a "null Recipe" means that the previous version cannot be
reconstructed, then the body MUST be removed from the third
part of the DSN and a `text/rfc822-headers` MIME part MUST
be supplied.

The resultant DSN is sent to the MAIL FROM address from the now
highest numbered DKIM2-Signature header field. The DSN itself
MUST have only one DKIM2-Signature field and only one
Message-Instance header field: i.e. it is a new message.

Following these procedures will ensure that no details
of where the message was forwarded to will be revealed to
the previous hop and the previous hop will be able to
apply the same procedure to return the DSN to its previous
hop, if that is required.

## Authentication of inbound DSNs

When a system receives a DKIM2 signed DSN, and the included
original message is also DKIM2 signed, it SHOULD verify that
the returned message (or just the header fields if the body
is not present) has not been altered.

This means:

1. There is a relaxed domain match (see {{relaxed-domain-match}})
between the Signing Domain of the i=1 DKIM2-Signature header field
of the DSN and a recipient address of the returned message.
Recipient addresses are located in the rt= tag of the
last (highest i= tag) DKIM2-Signature in the returned message.

1. The last (highest i= tag) DKIM2-Signature header field of the
returned message will be one that was generated by the system
receiving the DSN, determined by examining the
d= and mf= tags of that DKIM2-Signature header field.

1. The header fields of the embedded message (in the message/rfc822
MIME part) can be verified by checking the header hash values in
the highest numbered Message-Instance header field. If the message
body is present then that can be verified by inspecting the body
hash value in the highest numbered Message-Instance header field.

If the verification fails then the DSN MUST NOT be propagated
any further. If verification has been performed prior to
accepting the DSN from the sender the DSN SHOULD be rejected
with a 550/5.7.x return code. If the verification cannot be completed
because of a temporary issue (with DNS lookups) then a 4xx
return code SHOULD be used.

# Preventing transport conversions {#signer_normalize}

DKIM2's design is predicated on valid input.

In order to be signed a message MUST be in "network normal" format,
so that lines are separated with CRLF and any transfer encoding
has been applied.

A message that is not compliant with [RFC5322], [RFC2045], [RFC2047]
and other relevant message format standards can be subject to attempts
by intermediaries to correct or interpret such content.  See Section 8
of [RFC6409] for examples of changes that are commonly made.  Such
"corrections" may invalidate DKIM2 signatures or have other undesirable
effects, including some that involve changes to the way a message is
presented to an end user.

When calculating the hash on messages that will be transmitted using
base64 or quoted-printable encoding, Signers MUST compute the hash
after the encoding, and Verifiers will check the hash of the encoded
form of text. However, the hash MUST be computed before transport-level
encodings such as SMTP "dot-stuffing" (the modification of lines
beginning with a "." to avoid confusion with the SMTP end-of-message
marker, as specified in [RFC5321]).

Further, if the message contains local encoding that will be modified before transmission,
that modification to canonical [RFC5322] form MUST be done before signing.
In particular, bare CR or LF characters (used by some systems as a local line
separator convention) MUST be converted to the SMTP-standard CRLF
sequence before the message is signed.  Any conversion of this sort
MUST be applied to the message actually sent to the recipient(s),
not just to the version presented to the signing algorithm.

More generally, the Signer MUST sign the message as it is expected to
be received by the Verifier rather than in some local or internal form.

# EAI ([RFC6530]) considerations for DKIM2 {#EAI}

SMTPUTF8 lets MTAs send and receive non-ASCII characters. For the most
part DKIM2 works only in ASCII. Domain values used in the SMTP protocol
(MAIL FROM and RCPT TO) MUST be mapped from U-labels to A-labels before
being recorded in DKIM2-Signature header fields or used in comparisons (see
[RFC5890] and [RFC5891]). Local parts are recorded unchanged (the base64
encoding means no difficulties will arise).

Non-ASCII characters can also appear in From header fields, which are
recorded and compared when checking if they are Authentic. Again, a
U-label must be mapped to an A-label before comparisons are made. Local
parts are only ever compared in exact matches.

It should be noted that although email standards generally require
messages to be transported in simple ASCII (and provide numerous
encoding mechanisms to ensure this can be done) nevertheless messages
may contain non-ASCII characters. This is irrelevant for hashing
functions which treat messages as a simple array of bytes, but it
does have an impact on Recipes (see {{JSONrecipe}}). Accordingly,
Recipes handle header fields and body lines as being in UTF-8 (a
superset of ASCII) and provide "u" steps within Recipes for handling
sequences of byte values that are not valid UTF-8.

# IANA considerations

TBA -- we will need to document the two new header fields
There is limited value in registering tags, flags, hash and
signature algorithms, extensions will need new RFCs (and considerable
justification) not changes to a registry

# Security considerations

Unsigned header fields can be added or removed without detection, and so
they cannot safely be relied upon for security decisions. In particular,
systems may make decisions about what type of message is being handled by
looking for an Auto-Submitted header field, but this is a trace header
field and thus it is not signed. Additionally, conclusions may be drawn about
whether email is authentic based on Authentication-Results header fields
which are also unsigned. In the latter case, of course, a Validly Signed
DKIM2 Message provides unambiguous information about authenticity.

Although a DKIM2 message may only have a single Message-Instance header
field and just one DKIM2-Signature field, this specification allows for
many such header fields, multiple hashes and signatures and places no
limits on the complexity of Recipes. Even if limits were set,
malicious messages could ignore them. [RFC4732] surveys denial-of-service
attacks and provides guidance as to how to address them. Implementers
should take notice of this document and much other advice.

If a message has a null Recipe then it will be impossible to verify
the nature of any modifications made to the message when the null Recipe
was added. The expectation is that null Recipes will only
be used when there is a contractual relationship between the operators
doing the modification (perhaps a security gateway of some kind) and
the next recipient of the message. It is unlikely that this contractual
relationship will extend any further, so attempting to forward the
message any further could be unsuccessful.

This specification requires RSA keys to be at least 1024 bits. Longer
keys may be more appropriate in the future, and the algorithms
specified here may, in the course of time, become deprecated. Signers
should pay attention to community advice as to whether cryptographic
primitives are appropriate to use, regardless of what this specification
might say.

